          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been tired and light-headed for months, my heart races on stairs, and my periods are heavy. Is that urgent, what could iron deficiency do to the blood, and which ICD-10 chapter covers anemia?",
          "answer": "It is important to seek medical advice regarding your symptoms of tiredness, light-headedness, and a racing heart. Although these symptoms are assessed as routine urgency, if they are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, professional consultation is crucial for appropriate evaluation.\n\nIron deficiency can lead to anemia, which impacts the blood by reducing the concentration of hemoglobin—the protein responsible for carrying oxygen in red blood cells. A lack of iron prevents the production of sufficient healthy hemoglobin, resulting in smaller and paler red blood cells, known as microcytic, hypochromic anemia. This condition can manifest symptoms such as fatigue, breathlessness, palpitations, and dizziness, as the body struggles to oxygenate tissues effectively.\n\nAnemia is classified under Chapter 3 of the ICD-10, which is dedicated to diseases of the blood and blood-forming organs. This chapter includes various codes for types of anemia, including those for iron deficiency anemia.\n\nPlease make arrangements to discuss your symptoms and concerns with a healthcare professional, as they can provide the necessary evaluations and guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
